<h1>elastic-bucket trie</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A variant of a <a href="bucketTrie.html"><em>bucket trie</em></a> in which each <a href="leaf.html"><em>leaf</em></a> node for n strings is a <a href="bucket.html"><em>bucket</em></a> allocated to hold exactly n strings.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="bucketTrie.html"><em>bucket trie</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The name comes from re<strong>TRIE</strong>val and is pronounced, "tree".  See the historical note at <a href="trie.html"><em>trie</em></a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Thomas Papadakis</strong>, <em>Skip Lists and Probabilistic Analysis of Algorithms</em>, PhD Thesis, Faculty of Mathematics, University of Waterloo, Canada, May 1993, page 106.
</p>

